---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="The ARM9 menu pool"
  description="The little action verbs that appear on the inventory menu — Hold, Place, Bury, Equip — are stored directly in the game's main binary with strict byte budgets."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Names & Words', href: `${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/` },
    { label: 'ARM9 menu pool' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    When you tap an item in your inventory, a little menu pops up
    with verbs like "Hold," "Place," "Equip," "Bury." Those words
    aren't in the message dialog files. They're hardcoded into the
    game's main binary, in a fixed-size pool with a pointer table
    addressing each entry.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are 26 strings in this ARM9 pool, plus another 20 in a
    similar pool inside one of the overlays (overlay 96 — the NPC
    interaction menu), plus a handful of single-string overlays. We
    track 50 of these strings in total.
  </p>

  <h2>How they're stored</h2>
  <p>
    Each string sits in a fixed-size slot. The slot is sized so that
    even the longest reasonable Japanese version of the word fits.
    The text is UTF-16 with a NUL terminator. Once you account for
    the terminator, the budget in English characters is
    <code>(slot bytes / 2) - 1</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    A few slots and their budgets:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Slot bytes</th><th>JP text</th><th>EN budget</th><th>English version</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>もつ</td><td>3 chars</td><td>"Hold" doesn't fit; "Get" does</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>すてる</td><td>3 chars</td><td>"Toss" or "Drop"</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12</td><td>くちぶえ</td><td>5 chars</td><td>"Whistle" overflows; "Call" works</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12</td><td>みにつける</td><td>5 chars</td><td>"Equip" fits exactly</td></tr>
      <tr><td>16</td><td>そうびはずす</td><td>7 chars</td><td>"Unequip" fits exactly</td></tr>
      <tr><td>28</td><td>１０００００リッチ</td><td>13 chars</td><td>"100,000 Mira" at 12 chars</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The overflow problem</h2>
  <p>
    Most English words are longer than the equivalent Japanese. Of the
    50 strings in this pool, most of the natural English translations
    overflow their slot. We have three options for each one:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Shorten the English. "Bury" instead of the proper "Inter,"
      "Toss" instead of "Discard."</li>
    <li>Expand the slot. This means moving the string somewhere with
      more room and updating the pointer table that addresses it.
      Doable but invasive.</li>
    <li>Live with the overflow and accept rendering bugs. Not really
      an option for shipping.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The translation canon page tracks each slot's chosen approach.
    Most are option 1 (just shorten).
  </p>

  <h2>The currency tier strings</h2>
  <p>
    Five of the 26 slots are currency tier labels —
    <code>１リッチ</code>, <code>１０リッチ</code>,
    <code>１００リッチ</code>, <code>１０００リッチ</code>,
    <code>１００００リッチ</code>. These appear in the player's wallet
    display.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Japanese uses "リッチ" (Ricchi) as the currency unit. Game 1's
    English used <strong>Mira</strong> instead — a totally different
    word. There's no English in any binary, so the currency tier
    strings need fresh translation. The format is constrained: each
    slot grows by 4 bytes per tier, so the budget gets a little
    bigger as the numbers do.
  </p>

  <p>
    See <a href={`${base}/field-guide/translation-canon/`}>translation
    canon</a> for what we've settled on so far.
  </p>

  <h2>Where Game 1 helps</h2>
  <p>
    Of the 26 ARM9 strings, <strong>16 of them are word-for-word
    matches with strings in Magician's Quest 1's English release</strong>.
    That gives us official Konami translations to anchor our choices:
    "Hold," "Place," "Bury," "Discard," "Exit," "Next," "Back,"
    "Whistle," "Equip," "Unequip," "Release insect," "Release fish,"
    "Remove hat," "Remove glasses," "Change wallpaper," "Change
    flooring."
  </p>

  <p>
    The other 10 strings are Tongari-specific (the currency tiers, the
    "Save" / "Log" verb that has no Game 1 parallel, etc.) and need
    fresh translation. The overlay-96 NPC menu pool is entirely
    fresh — none of its 20 strings have Game 1 equivalents because
    Game 1 didn't have NPC interaction menus.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
